From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] GS CPU backend: triangle edge tests four pixels at a time, vector interpolation

What it is for:
  In Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level the GS is the limit. On a
  replay of the level's GS commands (perf), DrawTriangle's own code was
  about 17% of the time: per pixel of the bounding box, the three edge
  weights and the test, then per covered pixel the colour, S/T/Q and fog
  interpolated channel by channel.

The change:
  - With SSE2 (every x86-64 compiler) the weights w0, w1, w2 and the edge
    test are computed for four pixels of a row at once, with the scalar
    expressions' single-precision operations in the same order; a pixel
    is skipped exactly when one weight is below -kEdgeEpsilon (NaN
    weights are not, as before). The covered pixels then go through the
    same per-pixel code (now a lambda, shadePixel).
  - In shadePixel the four colour channels and S, T, Q are interpolated
    as vectors, a0 * w0 + a1 * w1 + a2 * w2 in that order; the colour is
    truncated and clamped to 0-255 by saturating packs, the same as
    clampU8(int(...)) (the truncating conversion gives INT_MIN for NaN and
    out-of-range values, like the scalar cast on x86).
  Other targets keep the scalar loop. Same results bit for bit.

How it was checked:
  The project's GS replay (tools/gs_replay, findings/20 s.24): the 15 s
  recording of the first level gives the same GS memory hashes at all 35
  points and at the end, and three synthetic recordings of 40,000 random
  records each (every primitive type, colour, Z and texture format, wrap
  mode, filter, blend and test state) give the same hashes at 20 points
  each with this patch, without it, without 0040 and without 0039. Level
  replay, best of 3, two rounds: 11.0-11.6 s -> 9.95-10.4 s. ps2xTest
  454/454.

--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/gs/gs_cpu_backend.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/gs/gs_cpu_backend.cpp
@@ -325,6 +325,31 @@
     // byte, with the same single-precision operations in the same order
     // (no fused multiply-add), so the same result bit for bit. The
     // per-channel calls were about a quarter of a GS band's time.
+#if PS2_GS_BILINEAR_SIMD
+    // a0 * w0 + a1 * w1 + a2 * w2 per lane, in that order: DrawTriangle's
+    // interpolation of four values at once.
+    inline __m128 interpolate4(__m128 a0, __m128 a1, __m128 a2, float w0, float w1, float w2)
+    {
+        return _mm_add_ps(_mm_add_ps(_mm_mul_ps(a0, _mm_set1_ps(w0)), _mm_mul_ps(a1, _mm_set1_ps(w1))),
+                          _mm_mul_ps(a2, _mm_set1_ps(w2)));
+    }
+
+    // clampU8(int(...)) of each lane, packed as RGBA bytes: the truncating
+    // conversion gives INT_MIN for NaN and out-of-range values, like the
+    // scalar cast on x86, and the saturating packs clamp to 0-255.
+    inline uint32_t interpolateBytes4(__m128 a0, __m128 a1, __m128 a2, float w0, float w1, float w2)
+    {
+        const __m128i v = _mm_cvttps_epi32(interpolate4(a0, a1, a2, w0, w1, w2));
+        const __m128i zero = _mm_setzero_si128();
+        return static_cast<uint32_t>(_mm_cvtsi128_si32(_mm_packus_epi16(_mm_packs_epi32(v, zero), zero)));
+    }
+
+    inline void interpolateFloats4(__m128 a0, __m128 a1, __m128 a2, float w0, float w1, float w2, float (&out)[4])
+    {
+        _mm_storeu_ps(out, interpolate4(a0, a1, a2, w0, w1, w2));
+    }
+#endif
+
